6431011 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534652 17592186045417 true] [17592186431011 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4.$" 13194139534652 17592186045417 true] [17592186431011 1072 #uuid "63a61ce2-0cf2-5d16-90c2-21d6b64fbbcd" 13194139534652 17592186045417 true] [17592186431011 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =4.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{2}{5}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534652 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-3fd03cb4-06c8-5ca2-9f08-a74b431e7f8f" 17592186431005 "answer-411fd766-6b79-5313-9896-cd722545c888" 17592186431006 "answer-4a13fd92-b1e8-58ed-960f-c91130469a89" 17592186431007 "answer-6387384c-e9f6-53af-81e8-9b9f29787d2f" 17592186431008 "answer-f0a25b95-c8b3-5a75-914b-884c50597eba" 17592186431009 "answer-field-3fe938d8-0558-5523-ac93-9554d3b69d6e" 17592186431010 "question-63a61ce2-0cf2-5d16-90c2-21d6b64fbbcd" 17592186431011} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
